 "standard_text": "Divide within 100 fluently, using the relationship between multiplication and division.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 3 division worksheets: equal sharing, division facts ÷2 to ÷10, fact families and missing numbers. 100 practice questions with step-by-step hints and a printable answer key.",
 "key_big": "To divide, think multiplication: 56 ÷ 8 = ? means 8 × ? = 56.",
 "key_sub": "Division splits a total into equal groups. If you know your times tables, you already know your division facts.",
 "body_html": "<h2>What division means</h2><p>Division splits a total into <b>equal groups</b>. <b>12 ÷ 3</b> can mean “share 12 into 3 equal groups” (4 in each group) or “how many groups of 3 are in 12?” (4 groups).</p><figure class='sn-fig'><svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 180 140' width='180' height='140' role='img' aria-label='array of 3 rows and 4 columns'><circle cx='30' cy='30' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='70' cy='30' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='110' cy='30' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='150' cy='30' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='30' cy='70' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='70' cy='70' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='110' cy='70' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='150' cy='70' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='30' cy='110' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='70' cy='110' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='110' cy='110' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='150' cy='110' r='13' fill='#006633'/></svg><figcaption>12 dots in 3 equal rows: 12 ÷ 3 = 4. The same array shows 3 × 4 = 12.</figcaption></figure><h2>Use multiplication to divide</h2><p>Every division fact has a <b>multiplication partner</b>. To solve 56 ÷ 8, ask: <b>8 times what is 56?</b> Since 8 × 7 = 56, the answer is 7.</p><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Fact family</th><th>Multiplication</th><th>Division</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3, 4, 12</td><td>3 × 4 = 12 &nbsp; 4 × 3 = 12</td><td>12 ÷ 3 = 4 &nbsp; 12 ÷ 4 = 3</td></tr><tr><td>6, 7, 42</td><td>6 × 7 = 42 &nbsp; 7 × 6 = 42</td><td>42 ÷ 6 = 7 &nbsp; 42 ÷ 7 = 6</td></tr><tr><td>8, 9, 72</td><td>8 × 9 = 72 &nbsp; 9 × 8 = 72</td><td>72 ÷ 8 = 9 &nbsp; 72 ÷ 9 = 8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>48 ÷ 6 = ?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Ask: 6 × ? = 48. 6 × 8 = 48.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 8</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Word problem</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>24 stickers are shared equally by 4 friends. How many does each friend get?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>24 ÷ 4 = ? → 4 × 6 = 24.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 6 stickers</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Switching the order (3 ÷ 12)</td><td>Start with the total: 12 ÷ 3</td></tr><tr><td>Subtracting instead (12 ÷ 3 = 9)</td><td>Division is equal groups, not taking away once</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking 5 ÷ 1 = 1</td><td>One group gets all 5: 5 ÷ 1 = 5</td></tr><tr><td>Dividing by 0</td><td>You cannot divide by 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you explain division to a 3rd grader?",
   "a": "Use equal sharing: 15 cookies shared by 3 friends gives each friend 5. Then connect it to multiplication: 3 × 5 = 15."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is a fact family?",
   "a": "A fact family is a set of three numbers linked by two multiplication facts and two division facts, such as 4 × 6 = 24, 6 × 4 = 24, 24 ÷ 4 = 6 and 24 ÷ 6 = 4."
  },
  {
   "q": "Should kids memorize division facts separately?",
   "a": "No. If a child knows the multiplication facts, each division fact is just the matching multiplication fact read backward."
  }
 ],
